The openness to risk is partially age-related. Adolescents and young adults seek risks out of the powerful need for peer acceptance and approval, self-presentation and self-esteem. They seek many risks in road traffic (e.g., drunk driving [14] and speeding) and are prone to phone use while driving [15,16]. Consequently, it is not surprising that young drivers are a vulnerable group in road traffic, motivating the present study to focus on phone use and distracted driving in young drivers. Technology continues advancing with smartphones as new means of communication and new instant messaging applications gain popularity, offering alternative ways to answer messages from photos over videos to voice messages. For answering messages while driving, voice-based input especially seems to be attractive as it offers seemingly less (visual and manual) distraction than text-based input at the human–machine interface and could be perceived as less intrusive, because instead of typing out the message, drivers record the spoken out message via a button push. Previous research already looked at the benefits of using voice-based systems while driving to control navigation systems or send emails. Although benefits of voice-based input compared with visual-manual input can be found, voice-based input still presents a critical distraction [17–20], leading to unsafe driving behaviors. Nevertheless, research comparing the effects of voice and text messaging is still very limited. Choudhary and Velaga [9] compared phone conversations and text messages with different complexities and found text messages to be more distracting. Studies further looked at the effects of listening to and answering voice messages [21–23] or showed imperfect voice recognition as a major cause of distraction and detrimental effects on driving [24]. Most of these studies compared messages read aloud or phone conversations with text messages, more or less dividing different answering modes. In many countries, the use of handheld devices (such as mobile phones and tablets) while driving is illegal. Often, it is not a matter for legislation but for law enforcement authorities to detect and take action against the use of handheld mobile activity and the legal compliance of drivers. An increasing number of drivers still illegally use handheld communication devices while driving (e.g., [25,26]). Voice-based and hands-free devices offer an alternative and legal way of navigation, entertainment and communication while driving. Still, even those devices require a minimum of manual and visual action control, such as for pushing a button or glancing at the map of a navigation system. To investigate this kind of driver–device interaction in a controlled setting, the present study focuses on the explicit functionality of a voice-based answering mode as an alternative and maybe safer response method while driving compared with a visual-manual (i.e., text-based) answering mode. The task in this study was to respond either vocally (voice-based) or visual-manually (text-based) to a five-digit code visually presented on a display. Only a few studies have focused on comparing text and voice messages [27], showing the need for more research. Many studies have shown the effects of driver inattention on lane keeping [28,29], offering a valid measure. The impact of phone use on lane keeping is not so pronounced, with few studies reporting smaller effects [30]. Texting while driving shows a similar picture, with substantial effects on lane keeping [31] for phones with physical keyboards and touchscreens [32,33]. As a standardized tool, the Lane Change Task (LCT) was used in previous studies to assess the influence of phone use on lane keeping. Detrimental effects of texting on driving performance in the LCT were already found [34], even for different entry methods [32]. Furthermore, voice-based entry for in-vehicle information systems [18] or navigation systems [17] showed improved performance over manual entry but still posed a significant distraction and degraded performance in the LCT. In this study, the effect of distraction on driving performance will be looked at in lane keeping behavior as it offers a valid measure. Furthermore, earlier studies comparing phone conversation or voice messaging with text messaging, on the other hand, mainly relied on car-following tasks [22,27] or looked at driving violations [23]. Therefore, the measure of lane keeping in this study can build a clearer understanding concerning the impact of text- vs. voice-based answering on driving performance.


