3.1. Driving Performance
Table 1 shows a summary of the means and standard deviations for driving performance and the subjective measures. The mean of the two baseline runs was used as the baseline in the following analyses. Differences in the mean deviation across all runs, the four dual-tasks and the baseline were analyzed via repeated measures ANOVA. Since Mauchly’s test indicated a violation for the assumption of sphericity (χ2 (9) = 30.58, p < 0.001), the degrees of freedom were corrected using Greenhouse–Geisser estimates of sphericity (ε = 0.60 [44]). The omnibus F-test showed that the mean deviation differed significantly across runs (F (2.38,52.45) = 38.58, p < 0.001, η2 = 0.975). In comparison with baseline driving (M = 0.28, SD = 0.05), the participants drove worse when sending textbased answers, regardless of whether the task was simple (M = 0.53, SD = 0.12) or complex (M = 0.59, SD = 0.24), and when sending voice-based answers, regardless of whether the task was simple (M = 0.39, SD = 0.11) or complex (M = 0.54, SD = 0.21). Post hoc pairwise comparisons showed these differences to be significant for all runs when writing text-based answers during the simple (t(22) = 13.25, p < 0.001, d = −2.29) and the complex tasks (t(22) = 5.62, p < 0.001, d = −2.26), as well as when recording voice-based answers during the simple (t(22) = 11.92, p < 0.001, d = −1.01) and the complex tasks (t(22) = 6.63, p < 0.001, d = −1.23). Factorial repeated-measures ANOVA for just the dual-task runs showed a significant main effect for the answering mode (F(1,22) = 12.84, p = 0.002, η2 = 0.16) and a significant main effect for the task complexity (F(1,22) = 37.82, p < 0.01, η2 = 0.22). On average, the participants were able to drive better when sending voice answers (M = 0.47, SD = 0.18) than when sending text-based answers (M = 0.56, SD = 0.13) and when work


