3.2. Subjective Demand
Subjective demand, in terms of the scores in the NASA-TLX, was analyzed across all runs via repeated measures ANOVA. Mauchly’s test was significant (χ2 (9) = 24.89, p = 0.003), and therefore the Greenhouse–Geisser corrected values were used (ε = 0.73 [44]). The omnibus F-Test showed that scores significantly differed across runs (F(4,88) = 65.58, p < 0.001, η2 = 0.99). In comparison with baseline driving (M = 31.01, SD = 16.72), the participants perceived higher demands when sending text-based answers, regardless of whether performing a simple (M = 66.52, SD = 16.42) or complex task (M = 83.17, SD = 16.61), and when sending voice-based answers, regardless of whether performing a simple (M = 44.74, SD = 14.39) or complex task (M = 73.09, SD = 19.18). Post hoc pairwise comparisons showed this difference to be significant for all runs, both when writing textbased answers during the simple (t(22) = 7.9, p < 0.001, d = −2.16) and complex tasks (t(22) = 12.03, p < 0.001, d = −3.13) as well as when recording voice-based answers during the simple (t(22) = 3.78, p = 0.001, d = −0.95) and complex tasks (t(22) = 9.90, p < 0.001, d = −2.19). Factorial repeated-measures ANOVA for just the dual-task runs showed a significant main effect for the answering mode (F(1,22) = 27.87, p < 0.001, η2 = 0.22) and a significant main effect for task complexity (F(1,22) = 92.7, p < 0.001, η2 = 0.43). On average, the participants perceived driving when sending voice-based answers (M = 59.35, SD = 22.14) to be less demanding than when sending text-based answers (M = 75.46 SD = 18.18) and working on the simple task (M = 56.04, SD = 18.88) to be less demanding than working on the complex task (M = 78.77, SD = 18.34). Furthermore, a significant interaction effect was found (F(1,22) = 12.96, p = 0.002, η2 = 0.03). Post hoc comparisons showed that subjective demand differed significantly between voice- and text-based answering (t(22) = 6.52, p < 0.001, d = −1.33) when working on the simple task as well as during the complex task (t(22) = 2.87, p = 0.009 d = −0.61). When sending text answers, subjective demand differed significantly between the simple and complex tasks (t(22) = −9.31, p < 0.001, d = 1.01), as well as when sending voice answers (t(22) = −7.86, p < 0.001, d = 1.97).
